  "data": {
    "title": "Transition of the Functional Brain Network Related to Increasing Cognitive Demands",
    "text": "Node Definition\nTo define nodes we used a functional brain parcellation consisting of 264 functionally relevant ROIs defined on the basis of a meta-analysis (Power et al., 2011). The functional ROIs were modeled using the MarsBaR toolbox as 10 mm diameter spheres. We cross-validated our findings using the Automated Anatomical Labeling (AAL) atlas dividing the brain into 90 regions.\n\nFor each condition, time course was extracted from each region of Power ROIs; then, Pearson’s correlation coefficients were computed for each pair of ROIs. Fisher’s transformation was then used to convert correlation coefficients to normally distributed z-scores. This resulted in one correlation matrix (264 × 264) for each condition and each subject.\n\nFunctional Brain Networks\nWe focused on fully connected, weighted graphs with positive and negative weights. Binary networks in hub detection analysis were constructed by applying proportional thresholding retaining 5–15% of the strongest connections (step 1%).\n\nGraph Measures\nNetwork modularity was computed using the Louvain algorithm for both weighted and binary networks. The algorithm was run 1,000 times and the partition with the highest modularity Q was selected. Provincial and connector hubs were identified using within-module degree z-score and participation coefficient."
  },
